                <pb facs="0353" n="249" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg249"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4044: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-07T18:00:00+08:00">February 7, 1932—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 7—2:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>12. I have just been assured by Consul General Murai that the military force
                    arriving today would not be landed in the International Settlement. That it is
                    the intention to land at Woosung and he believed they had already landed at 5
                    o’clock. Refer to this office’s telegram No. 8 of February 6th, 5 p.m. Repeated
                    to the Legation, Nanking and Tokyo for information.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
